---
import DocsLayout from "../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
---

<DocsLayout title="Bernstein Background | DP-LMI Package">
  <section class="hero compact">
    <h1>Bernstein Background</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      DP-LMI objects are stored as local Bernstein coefficients over tensor
      grid cells. The same storage model supports numeric matrices and affine
      YALMIP decision expressions.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section two-col">
    <div>
      <h2>Cells And Local Labels</h2>
      <p>
        Each parameter dimension is split by grid nodes. Inside a cell, local
        coordinates map the physical interval to Bernstein coordinates. A
        degree-one scalar cell has two local labels; a two-dimensional
        degree-one tensor cell has four labels.
      </p>
    </div>
    <pre><code>{`A = dpmat({[0 1], [10 20]}, {1, 3; 5, 7}, Degree=1);
size(A.LocalValues{1,1})

ans =

     2     2`}</code></pre>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>Products Raise Degree</h2>
    <p>
      Multiplication is coefficient convolution with Bernstein normalization.
      A degree-one factor times a degree-one factor produces degree two.
      Current <code>dpmat</code> and supported mixed <code>dpmat</code>/<code>dpvar</code>
      products use this rule over common refined grids.
    </p>
    <pre><code>{`A = dpmat({[0 1]}, {1, 2}, Degree=1);
B = dpmat({[0 1]}, {10, 20}, Degree=1);
C = A * B;
C.Degree

ans =

     2`}</code></pre>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>Tensor Grids</h2>
    <p>
      For multiple parameters, coefficient labels are tensor-product labels.
      Rate-weighted derivatives enumerate admissible rate vertices from
      <code>RateBounds</code>, then <code>dplmi</code> constrains every cell,
      local coefficient, and rate row.
    </p>
  </section>
</DocsLayout>
